# `std::io::path_buf` — Normalized Path Component Builder (`PathBuf`)

## 1. Architectural Intent & Rust `std::path::PathBuf` Comparison
In Rust (`std::path::PathBuf`), `push("..")` appends a literal parent component without normalizing the path in-place unless `fs::canonicalize` is invoked with OS I/O side effects, which can expose path-traversal (`..`) vulnerabilities when constructing sandboxed relative paths.

In Jenny's `std::io::path_buf`, `PathBuf` maintains a normalized component stack backed by `std::mem::raw_buf::RawBuf` (`invariant self.depth >= 0 && self.depth <= self.buf.cap`), rejecting invalid or root-escaping traversal segments via explicit `requires ... else PathError` contracts.

```jenny
module std_io_path_buf

import std::mem::raw_buf

pub enum PathError {
    EmptyPath,
    InvalidSegment,
    TraversalEscape,
    CapacityOverflow,
}

pub struct PathBuf {
    buf: RawBuf,
    depth: i64 = 0,
    is_abs: i64 = 0,

    invariant self.depth >= 0 && self.depth <= self.buf.cap && (self.is_abs == 0 || self.is_abs == 1)
}

impl PathBuf {
    pub fn new(is_abs: i64) -> PathBuf ! PathError
        effects []
        requires is_abs == 0 || is_abs == 1 else PathError.InvalidSegment
        ensures result.depth == 0 && result.is_abs == is_abs && result.buf.cap == 0

    pub fn depth(self) -> i64
        effects []
        ensures result == self.depth && result >= 0

    pub fn push_segment(mut self, seg_id: i64) -> i64 ! PathError
        effects [Mutate, AllocNative]
        requires seg_id > 0 else PathError.InvalidSegment
        requires self.depth < 65536 else PathError.CapacityOverflow
        ensures self.depth == old(self.depth) + 1 && result == self.depth

    pub fn pop_segment(mut self) -> i64 ! PathError
        effects [Mutate]
        requires self.depth > 0 else PathError.EmptyPath
        ensures self.depth == old(self.depth) - 1

    pub fn normalize_parent(mut self) -> i64 ! PathError
        effects [Mutate]
        requires self.depth > 0 else PathError.TraversalEscape
        ensures self.depth == old(self.depth) - 1 && result == self.depth

    pub fn leaf_segment(self) -> i64 ! PathError
        effects []
        requires self.depth > 0 else PathError.EmptyPath

    pub fn drop_path(mut self) -> i64
        effects [Mutate, AllocNative]
        ensures self.depth == 0 && self.buf.cap == 0 && result == 0
}

@example("EX-STD-PATH-01: PathBuf push_segment, leaf_segment, normalize_parent, pop_segment, and drop") {
    var p = PathBuf.new(1)!
    assert(p.depth() == 0)
    assert(p.is_abs == 1)
    p.push_segment(10)!
    p.push_segment(20)!
    p.push_segment(30)!
    assert(p.leaf_segment()! == 30)
    p.normalize_parent()!
    assert(p.leaf_segment()! == 20)
    val popped = p.pop_segment()!
    assert(popped == 20)
    assert(p.depth() == 1)
    p.drop_path()
    assert(active_native_boxes() == 0)
}

@example("EX-STD-PATH-02: PathBuf blocks root traversal escape and invalid segments") {
    var p = PathBuf.new(0)!
    val e1 = p.normalize_parent()
    assert(e1 is Err(PathError.TraversalEscape))
    val e2 = p.pop_segment()
    assert(e2 is Err(PathError.EmptyPath))
    val e3 = p.push_segment(0)
    assert(e3 is Err(PathError.InvalidSegment))
    val e4 = p.push_segment(-5)
    assert(e4 is Err(PathError.InvalidSegment))
    p.drop_path()
    assert(active_native_boxes() == 0)
}
```
